.TH LONGWIRE-PROFILES 5 "October 2026" "longwire 0.5.18" "longwire manual"
.SH NAME
longwire-profiles \- saved longwire connections
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I ~/.config/longwire/profiles
.SH DESCRIPTION
A profile is a saved connection: which switchboard, the token it asked for, how to attach, and so on.
The file holds all of them.
.BR longwire (1),
the Control Panel pane and the menu bar item all read it, so a setting is made in one place.
.PP
Because it holds tokens, the directory is mode 0700 and the file mode 0600.
A token is an invitation to register once, not an identity; the identity (the seed) is kept elsewhere and never enters this file.
Anyone who can read your home directory can read your tokens.
.PP
The file can be edited by hand.
.B longwire profile
rewrites it whole, so a comment you add is not kept.
.SH FORMAT
A line
.B default
.I name
names the default profile.
A line
.B profile
.I name
starts a profile; the indented lines that follow belong to it.
Each is
.IB key " = " value
and the value runs to the end of the line.
A profile name is letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens.
.PP
.nf
.RS
default home

profile home
  switchboard = vps.example.org:1877/lw1...
  token = 5fe1c0ab...
  attach = jack
  interface = lw0
  address = 10.77.0.2/24
  name = osakka
  listed = no
  answer = known
.RE
.fi
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B switchboard
.IR HOST:PORT/KEY :
the switchboard's address, a slash, and its full address
.RB ( lw1... )
or 64 hex digits.
.TP
.B token
The registration token, for a switchboard that wants one.
Needed the first time a machine registers; after that the switchboard knows its key and the token can be removed.
.TP
.B attach
.B jack
(a tap device for one machine) or
.B splice
(a NIC, for a whole site).
.TP
.B interface
The tap device (default
.BR lw0 )
or the NIC to splice.
.TP
.B address
For a jack: this machine's address on the line, like 10.77.0.2/24, put on the device for you when the node is turned on.
Every machine on a line needs its own, in one subnet.
The word
.B dhcp
instead asks a DHCP server on the line, through a client (dhclient, dhcpcd or udhcpc) that the same step starts; it takes the address and the network route only, never a default route or name servers.
Empty leaves the device alone.
.TP
.B name
The name announced to the nodes you call.
A claim, never proof.
.TP
.B listed
.B yes
to be found by name in the switchboard's directory; default
.BR no .
.TP
.B answer
.BR ask ,
.BR known ,
.B auto
or
.BR never :
the answer policy.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/longwire/profiles
The file.
.B LW_CONF
names its directory instead.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR longwire (1),
.BR longwire-switchboard (1),
.BR longwire-security (7)
